    {
      "index": "omz-mm-text",
      "component_type": "text",
      "title": "Making two acquisitions coincide",
      "workflow_tag": "",
      "data_collection_tag": "",
      "wf_id": {
        "$oid": "646b0f3c1e4a2d7f8e5b8f01"
      },
      "dc_id": null,
      "dc_config": {},
      "cols_json": {},
      "parent_index": null,
      "last_updated": "2026-05-12T00:00:00.000000",
      "title_align": "left",
      "section": "Registration",
      "order": 6,
      "alignment": "left",
      "vertical_alignment": "top",
      "body": "The same breast tissue core, imaged twice: in bright field (an H&E stained section, nuclei purple, stroma pink) and by second-harmonic generation, which shows only the collagen fibres. The second image was rotated and shifted (up to 30 degrees and 100 px), as a second instrument would leave it. The viewer shows it, in magenta, after each step of a registration that brings it into the bright-field frame, with the bright field faded over it: where the two coincide, the fibres sit on the pink stroma. The steps are a real itk-elastix run, mutual information as the metric since the techniques share no intensities. A fit on bright-field brightness fails (brightness says little about collagen); a fit on what the stain says of the stroma (eosin minus haematoxylin), from seven start rotations, lands 1B_B8 and 2B_E4 within a few pixels of the truth. 1B_J2 stays off by about 100 px, and the corner error says so: the dataset gives where the image truly belongs. Data from Eliceiri, Li and Keikhosravi, Multimodal Biomedical Dataset for Evaluating Registration Methods (Zenodo 3874362, CC BY 4.0), from Conklin et al., Am J Pathol 2011.\n",
      "surface": "none",
      "accent": null,
      "logo": null,
      "logo_dark": null,
      "wf_tag": "python/bioimage_demo"
    },
